1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
Pliable, durable, stable, strong.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:11,000
These specially formulated racing tires have precise adhesive qualities

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
and are the only way to connect 8,000 horsepower to the ground.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Tough fuel dragsters have about 43 times more power than an average passenger car

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:27,000
and can accelerate from 0 to 100 miles per hour in less than 8 tenths of a second.

6
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:30,120
Only if the high-tech tires perform perfectly.

7
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,740
One of the most important things in racing is obviously rubber.

8
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:40,540
And that's also one of the biggest secrets in racing because that's basically where most of your speed comes from.

9
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,620
Designed to stretch like a rubber band, these tires grow in diameter as centrifugal force increases.

10
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:54,180
As the tire is sitting static on the race car before he launches off the line, you'll probably have about this much sitting on the racetrack.

11
00:00:54,181 --> 00:01:04,727
As they go down track at, say, 250 miles an hour, the tire will actually stand up and act as an overdrive gear so that the race car can actually keep pulling as it

12
00:01:04,728 --> 00:01:06,280
goes down the racetrack.

13
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:13,900
We use our motorsports to advance our technology, and every car that races is really a moving laboratory.

14
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:22,160
Dragsters, sports cars, and stock cars provide valuable research to the manufacturers of the tires found on everyday cars and trucks.

15
00:01:22,161 --> 00:01:26,180
tires are the most widespread use of rubber

16
00:01:26,181 --> 00:01:29,900
over 1.3 billion tires are made each year

17
00:01:29,901 --> 00:01:33,560
and account for 68% of all rubber production

18
00:01:33,561 --> 00:01:38,920
in total the world consumes 20 million tons of rubber annually

19
00:01:38,921 --> 00:01:44,060
some experts contend that rubber is the fourth most critical resource in the world

20
00:01:44,061 --> 00:01:45,140
after air

21
00:01:45,141 --> 00:01:47,000
water

22
00:01:47,001 --> 00:01:48,960
and oil

23
00:01:48,961 --> 00:01:52,340
and perhaps the most fragile.

24
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:58,300
Rubber, as we know it, and as it's grown in plantations, is Hevea brasiliensis.

25
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:00,880
Hevea is a genus in the family Euphorbiaceae.

26
00:02:00,881 --> 00:02:06,640
The Euphorbiaceae family typically has latex in the various species.

27
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:11,480
The latex is a protective mechanism against insect predation.

28
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:13,920
Of course, the insects chew into the plant,

29
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:18,600
and the latex, being a gummy substance, will clog up their mouth parts,

30
00:02:18,601 --> 00:02:20,360
and protect the plant.

31
00:02:20,940 --> 00:02:23,220
Found in rainforest regions around the equator,

32
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:26,660
the delicate latex that oozes from beneath the bark

33
00:02:26,661 --> 00:02:28,460
is the base for rubber compounds.

34
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:32,400
The challenge comes, how do you get to the latex

35
00:02:32,401 --> 00:02:35,580
without cutting into the cambia layer of the plant,

36
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:37,500
which is a living growth layer of the plant?

37
00:02:37,501 --> 00:02:41,300
If you cut too deeply in, you will actually hurt,

38
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:42,700
if not destroy, the plant.

39
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:44,740
And just by going into the bark layer,

40
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:46,400
just into the latex vessels,

41
00:02:46,401 --> 00:02:49,860
you'll get this sort of beautiful milk coming out,

42
00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:52,060
which is often called the white blood of the forest.

43
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:58,940
Latex extraction dates all the way back to 1600 B.C.

44
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:01,660
and the Mesoamerican Olmec civilization,

45
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:04,480
which loosely translates to rubber people.

46
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:07,740
The Olmecs played a sophisticated game with a latex ball,

47
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:11,620
made by dripping the gooey substance into boiling water.

48
00:03:11,621 --> 00:03:24,160
They took the liquid that came from the Hevia tree, they were able to let this dry, roll it into a ball and then it would bounce.

49
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:29,140
And so it was a great plaything at that particular time.

50
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:38,500
That's the only thing that people really knew about rubber was that it was no particular economic value.

51
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:40,600
During the 16th century,

52
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:44,120
Spanish and Portuguese conquerors of South America

53
00:03:44,121 --> 00:03:47,240
documented the many uses the indigenous tribes had developed

54
00:03:47,241 --> 00:03:48,740
for the white blood of the forest.

55
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:56,420
They found that they would coat, you know, their crude cloth

56
00:03:56,421 --> 00:04:00,700
with the same latex and it would become a waterproof covering.

57
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:03,660
It was latex that was dried on cloth.

58
00:04:04,340 --> 00:04:06,920
They made capes, they made rubber shoes,

59
00:04:06,921 --> 00:04:09,040
but all these products had a critical flaw.

60
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:12,200
In hot weather, a rubber cape would become a sticky shroud,

61
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:16,380
and in cold weather, a pair of rubber shoes would crack like porcelain.

62
00:04:17,020 --> 00:04:22,080
Despite the shortcomings, the sailors coerced the natives to milk more trees

63
00:04:22,081 --> 00:04:25,100
as the wondrous latex started to be shipped to Europe regularly.

64
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:30,840
However, the long passage would often result in spoilage and hardening of the product.

65
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:37,220
Until 1761, when Dr. François Freneau, a French scientist,

66
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:41,480
discovered that turpentine was an ideal solvent for the crude latex

67
00:04:41,481 --> 00:04:43,680
and revived its pliable properties.

68
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:47,220
This discovery opened the door to the global trade of raw rubber.

69
00:04:47,840 --> 00:04:55,960
By the early 19th century, Europeans and Americans understood that rubber had a consumer use.

70
00:04:55,961 --> 00:05:06,680
And so there were a lot of sort of inventors and chemists who were trying to find a way to make rubber a more viable product for a wide variety of consumer goods.

71
00:05:07,140 --> 00:05:15,980
Processing rubber began in earnest when Thomas Hancock built his Masticator in 1819, which shredded scrap rubber like a meat grinder.

72
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:24,420
Hancock learned that the mashing would warm the rubber into consistencies that could be shaped and would more easily mix with other materials.

73
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:30,560
This process paved the way for improved quality products from men like Charles McIntosh,

74
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:35,980
who in 1823 produced the first commercially viable raincoat, the Mac.

75
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:43,960
But the most significant breakthrough in rubber processing came quite by accident to a man named Charles Goodyear.

76
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:49,920
It happened in the privacy of his own kitchen, and it happened under the conditions of a beleaguered husband

77
00:05:49,921 --> 00:05:54,560
who didn't want his wife to know that he was still trying to make rubber useful for humans

78
00:05:54,561 --> 00:06:01,080
and somehow in the process accidentally made the right combinations of elements work.

79
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:05,020
The accident happened in how much he heated up the rubber,

80
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:11,780
and that's why vulcanization, Vulcan being the Roman god of fire, is the name of the process.

81
00:06:11,781 --> 00:06:15,900
When Goodyear introduced sulfur under heat and pressure to natural rubber,

82
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:19,540
It formed bridges between the strands of latex molecules

83
00:06:19,541 --> 00:06:24,240
and bonded them together in an unbreakable manner to give rubber its elastic properties.

84
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:29,780
It was only the accidental discovery of vulcanization by Charles Goodyear

85
00:06:29,781 --> 00:06:36,340
that transformed rubber from a curiosity to a fundamental component of the industrial age.

86
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,880
And that was an extraordinary discovery, and the consequences were profound.

87
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:44,880
Vulcanized rubber for shoe soles, dolls and toys,

88
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:47,120
Bicycle and carriage tires.

89
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:49,660
Telegraph and electric cable insulation.

90
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:54,580
At last, industry realized rubber had significant economic potential.

91
00:06:55,780 --> 00:06:58,240
Hoping to capitalize on the opportunities rubber offered,

92
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:01,160
the British set out to grow rubber within their colonies.

93
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:08,000
They dispatched Henry Wickham to disrupt the monopoly Brazil had on the valuable rubber resource.

94
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:15,660
The British managed to get some seeds out of Brazil and tried to propagate the heavier tree,

95
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:21,140
and they managed to get some of those trees to germinate from the seeds they took.

96
00:07:21,680 --> 00:07:27,960
And they started a rubber plantation business in Sri Lanka, or actually the island of Ceylon.

97
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:32,120
One of the things they discovered, for example, is that it was far more efficient,

98
00:07:32,121 --> 00:07:38,420
both in terms of productivity of latex and in terms of the speed of growth of a plantation

99
00:07:38,421 --> 00:07:45,480
to propagate the trees vegetatively, not by seed, but from cuttings, from vegetative cuttings of the plant.

100
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:52,340
Once the plantations came online, the reversal of fortunes was absolutely astonishing.

101
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:58,800
In time, these British-controlled plantations would become the primary suppliers of the world's rubber demands.

102
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:02,260
But during the last half of the 19th century,

103
00:08:02,860 --> 00:08:06,080
the real rubber fortunes were found in the heart of the Brazilian jungle.

104
00:08:06,780 --> 00:08:10,940
Where the Negro and Amazon rivers meet lies the city of Manaus,

105
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:14,760
a city of fantastic wealth

106
00:08:14,761 --> 00:08:18,840
and home to the plantation owners who controlled the world's rubber market.

107
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:21,740
They became known as the rubber barons.

108
00:08:23,720 --> 00:08:26,100
Manaus, which was the epicenter of the trade,

109
00:08:26,101 --> 00:08:29,640
had the highest per capita consumption of diamonds in the world,

110
00:08:29,820 --> 00:08:33,980
and prostitutes that came in from Marrakesh and Tangier and St. Petersburg

111
00:08:33,981 --> 00:08:37,800
would collect $7,000, $8,000 for an evening's work.

112
00:08:38,300 --> 00:08:42,240
Men would slake the thirst of their horses with chilled French champagne.

113
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:47,100
The matrons of the town of Manaus dispatched their laundry to Portugal

114
00:08:47,640 --> 00:08:50,120
because they disdained the murky waters of the Amazon.

115
00:08:50,121 --> 00:08:56,060
Now, all of this wealth was based on this tree of life, Hevia Braziliansis,

116
00:08:56,100 --> 00:09:00,320
A tree of life to some, a tree of suffering and death to others.

117
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:05,260
The arduous task of tapping the latex required an enormous labor force,

118
00:09:05,660 --> 00:09:09,540
one that was considered expendable by the landowners.

119
00:09:10,420 --> 00:09:16,340
And when the supply of manpower from the impoverished northeast of Brazil became exhausted,

120
00:09:16,860 --> 00:09:21,720
the rubber barons and their greed turned to the first inhabitants of the Amazon, the Indians.

121
00:09:21,721 --> 00:09:28,600
It was one thing to sort of secure in a form of bondage indentured labor, some impoverished peasant.

122
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,220
You know, you would give him a certain amount of goods.

123
00:09:31,700 --> 00:09:35,120
He would incur a certain level of debt from which he could never escape,

124
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:37,080
a debt that would be inherited through the generations.

125
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:37,980
That was one thing.

126
00:09:38,220 --> 00:09:39,220
But what about the Indians?

127
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:41,260
How do you force them to work?

128
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:47,540
Well, the rubber barons looked around for a reason, and they settled upon terror.

129
00:09:47,541 --> 00:09:51,780
and the atrocities that were unleashed at the height of the rubber boom

130
00:09:51,781 --> 00:10:00,000
are simply too dreadful and formidable to even begin to speak about them.

131
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:03,240
Little was ever said about these human tragedies.

132
00:10:04,080 --> 00:10:08,780
Outside the jungle, few were aware the plight of the natives was to be slave labor.

133
00:10:09,760 --> 00:10:14,700
Yet their sacrifices would facilitate the rapid expansion of rubber exports at the turn of the century.

134
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:17,280
It was a time rife with invention.

135
00:10:17,540 --> 00:10:19,540
innovation and imagination.

136
00:10:20,540 --> 00:10:22,540
It was the birth of a new industrial age

137
00:10:22,541 --> 00:10:25,540
and a time for a new means of transportation,

138
00:10:25,541 --> 00:10:26,540
the automobile,

139
00:10:26,541 --> 00:10:29,540
which would strain the world's rubber supply.

140
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:36,540
The name rubber is credited to the noted scientist Joseph Priestley,

141
00:10:36,541 --> 00:10:42,540
who in 1770 discovered that dried latex rubbed out or erased pencil marks.

142
00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:46,540
Rubber will return on Modern Marvels.

143
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:17,520
The Tire Industry

144
00:11:17,540 --> 00:11:22,071
of those. John Boyd Dunlop was an Irish veterinarian.

145
00:11:22,072 --> 00:11:26,260
He took a rubber tube and a valve from a football

146
00:11:26,261 --> 00:11:34,720
and he wrapped this thing around the wooden wheels on his son's bike and he pumped some air into it

147
00:11:34,721 --> 00:11:40,112
to make it more cushioned and in the process invented the pneumatic tire.

148
00:11:40,113 --> 00:11:41,220
Like so many of

149
00:11:41,221 --> 00:11:47,322
these stories, a lot of this was just invention by necessity. B.F.

150
00:11:47,323 --> 00:11:50,280
Goodrich was a Civil War surgeon

151
00:11:50,281 --> 00:11:56,164
who, after the war, was looking for his niche in the business world.

152
00:11:56,165 --> 00:11:58,500
He decided to establish his

153
00:11:58,501 --> 00:12:05,520
then fairly modest rubber company in Akron and stuck it out long enough, struggling much of the

154
00:12:05,521 --> 00:12:11,000
time, for the automobile to enter the picture, which of course changed the entire industry.

155
00:12:11,220 --> 00:12:18,820
And one myth about the tire industry that's probably easy to understand is that Charles Goodyear started the Goodyear Tire and Rubber Company.

156
00:12:19,220 --> 00:12:28,932
But in fact, Frank and Charles Seierling did start the Goodyear Tire and Rubber Company and named their company in honor of the man who had invented vulcanization that

157
00:12:28,933 --> 00:12:30,560
made their product possible.

158
00:12:30,561 --> 00:12:38,780
And the story of the O'Neill family's tire company, General Tire, is amazing in its own way

159
00:12:38,781 --> 00:12:45,580
because they succeeded not only in making tires, but understanding that in this very young industry,

160
00:12:45,780 --> 00:12:50,060
there were niches, and the niche that nobody had latched onto was replacement tires.

161
00:12:51,080 --> 00:12:53,960
Some tire makers forged strong bonds with car makers.

162
00:12:54,640 --> 00:13:01,800
Harvey Firestone was born a farm boy in Columbiana, Ohio, a very rural part of the state.

163
00:13:01,801 --> 00:13:12,760
He sort of was often portrayed as this sort of pull-yourself-up-by-the-bootstraps kind of guy who rose into this very blue-blood, very regal figure.

164
00:13:13,020 --> 00:13:19,940
He struck up a friendship with a young man named Henry Ford, and in fact it was over the issue of tires.

165
00:13:19,941 --> 00:13:25,240
Harvey Firestone moved to Akron and started the Firestone Tire and Rubber Company.

166
00:13:25,560 --> 00:13:30,500
His early friendship with Henry Ford would come back to pay off in spades

167
00:13:30,501 --> 00:13:36,280
because for years Firestone was the main supplier to the Ford Motor Company

168
00:13:36,281 --> 00:13:40,940
and that was both a personal and professional relationship

169
00:13:41,580 --> 00:13:44,340
that carried really across the 20th century.

170
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:49,340
Ford and Firestone were intensely aware of how critical and how fragile rubber was

171
00:13:49,341 --> 00:13:50,580
as a natural resource.

172
00:13:51,600 --> 00:13:54,040
As World War I put an enormous strain

173
00:13:54,041 --> 00:13:55,280
on the world rubber supplies,

174
00:13:56,080 --> 00:13:57,800
Ford decided to protect his interests

175
00:13:57,801 --> 00:14:01,260
by establishing Fordlandia in 1926.

176
00:14:02,180 --> 00:14:05,580
His 2.5 million acre parcel of northern Brazil

177
00:14:05,581 --> 00:14:07,660
first had to be cleared and terraced.

178
00:14:08,220 --> 00:14:09,820
Then seeds from the Hevia trees

179
00:14:09,821 --> 00:14:11,900
were planted across the valleys and hillsides.

180
00:14:12,460 --> 00:14:13,520
In just a few years,

181
00:14:13,860 --> 00:14:15,600
a thriving company town plantation

182
00:14:15,601 --> 00:14:17,120
supported thousands of workers.

183
00:14:18,120 --> 00:14:21,640
Beyond Ford's control, however, was the ever-present leaf blight,

184
00:14:22,100 --> 00:14:23,840
which decimated his fledgling crops.

185
00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:26,400
Fordlandia faltered and failed.

186
00:14:27,020 --> 00:14:31,500
So Ford created a second plantation, Belterra, in 1934.

187
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,800
While it outperformed Fordlandia, it was still not successful.

188
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,800
Better luck came to Ford's friend, Harvey Firestone,

189
00:14:39,801 --> 00:14:42,940
who focused his efforts toward a different area near the equator,

190
00:14:43,360 --> 00:14:45,100
on the continent of Africa.

191
00:14:45,101 --> 00:14:55,520
Harvey Firestone decided that he would make an agreement with Liberia and start a plantation to produce our own rubber, our own latex.

192
00:14:56,120 --> 00:14:59,360
That plantation is still in use today.

193
00:15:00,360 --> 00:15:06,420
At the height of its productivity, it had over 10,000 employees.

194
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:10,100
As it sits right now, we have over 8,000 employees

195
00:15:10,101 --> 00:15:15,380
that harvest the natural latex rubber that's used in our tires today.

196
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:18,620
While plantations were located all along the equator,

197
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,640
rubber production was dominated by those in Southeast Asia.

198
00:15:22,020 --> 00:15:25,760
But all were about to become pawns in a global game of military strategy.

199
00:15:27,040 --> 00:15:29,260
The sudden entry into World War II

200
00:15:29,261 --> 00:15:32,620
forced the U.S. government to set goals for rubber consumption

201
00:15:32,621 --> 00:15:35,160
that far exceeded natural rubber production.

202
00:15:36,420 --> 00:15:43,980
Rubber was wrapped around every mile of wiring in every military and every domestic installation in this country.

203
00:15:44,460 --> 00:15:46,820
Every Sherman tank had half a ton of rubber.

204
00:15:47,380 --> 00:15:52,240
Every battleship that was to be sunk at Pearl Harbor had over 20,000 rubber parts.

205
00:15:53,420 --> 00:16:02,020
After Pearl Harbor, the Japanese seized control of the massive rubber plantations in Southeast Asia, crippling the Allies' access to rubber supplies.

206
00:16:02,520 --> 00:16:04,840
It was an incredible crisis.

207
00:16:05,600 --> 00:16:12,880
At that moment, 95% of the world's rubber supply grew within 15 degrees longitude and latitude of Singapore,

208
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:15,480
the first place the Japanese planned to hit.

209
00:16:15,481 --> 00:16:21,680
The Japanese took the entire world's supply, leaving us without a vital commodity of the industrial age.

210
00:16:22,540 --> 00:16:27,480
Roosevelt set into motion three draconian initiatives that in wartime situation

211
00:16:28,180 --> 00:16:33,340
became really the great symbols of the spirit of American industry.

212
00:16:33,341 --> 00:16:38,220
the first thing that we did is set in motion the biggest recycling campaign in the history of the earth.

213
00:16:38,740 --> 00:16:41,720
Americans were asked to salvage and collect used rubber

214
00:16:41,721 --> 00:16:46,300
and a long list of other materials which could be repurposed for the war effort.

215
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:51,440
These scrap drives were the subject of great publicity in the early days of the war

216
00:16:51,441 --> 00:16:56,660
as a way for average citizens of all ages to support the country in its time of need.

217
00:16:57,860 --> 00:17:02,740
The second initiative required aid from citizens with expertise in natural resources.

218
00:17:03,340 --> 00:17:08,980
We sent plant explorers to every corner of the free world

219
00:17:08,981 --> 00:17:12,880
to squeeze every drop of latex we possibly could.

220
00:17:13,260 --> 00:17:17,420
Above everything, we sent plant explorers back into the heart of the Amazon,

221
00:17:17,940 --> 00:17:21,280
not only to secure new sources of latex,

222
00:17:21,281 --> 00:17:26,340
but more importantly, to find a way to forever free ourselves

223
00:17:26,341 --> 00:17:29,240
of dependency on distant plantations.

224
00:17:29,920 --> 00:17:33,180
But it was the third directive that made the most vital impact.

225
00:17:33,340 --> 00:17:37,240
a decree to manufacture artificial rubber.

226
00:17:38,260 --> 00:17:40,660
The order went out to the synthetic chemists

227
00:17:40,661 --> 00:17:43,320
that they had no choice but to create an industry.

228
00:17:43,860 --> 00:17:45,880
They were told that if by 1943

229
00:17:45,881 --> 00:17:49,540
you can't find a way to make a million metric tons

230
00:17:49,541 --> 00:17:52,860
of a product that has not even entered the developmental stages,

231
00:17:53,280 --> 00:17:54,420
the war will be lost.

232
00:17:54,640 --> 00:17:59,260
So a real push was made to develop our own synthetic rubber.

233
00:17:59,920 --> 00:18:03,300
The government actually nationalized many of the plants

234
00:18:03,301 --> 00:18:08,240
and took over and set priorities for the development work.

235
00:18:09,320 --> 00:18:11,880
And we were successful in doing that.

236
00:18:11,881 --> 00:18:16,940
The basic discoveries in the laboratory gave a good impetus to it,

237
00:18:17,040 --> 00:18:22,260
but it was the scale-up to production quantities needed for the war effort

238
00:18:22,261 --> 00:18:28,040
that really became almost a miracle of the same order as the Manhattan Project,

239
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:32,980
that is a government-industry-university research combine

240
00:18:32,981 --> 00:18:38,280
that really pushed as hard as it could to get somewhere in a very short period of time

241
00:18:38,281 --> 00:18:41,780
in the space of two years producing hundreds of thousands of tons

242
00:18:41,781 --> 00:18:44,780
of a quite usable synthetic rubber.

243
00:18:45,620 --> 00:18:46,360
Quite important.

244
00:18:47,360 --> 00:18:48,300
Synthetic rubber.

245
00:18:49,260 --> 00:18:52,680
The chemical manipulation of elements that produced a substance

246
00:18:52,681 --> 00:18:55,200
which emulated the properties of natural rubber.

247
00:18:57,140 --> 00:19:00,240
Experiments with synthetics predated the war by two decades.

248
00:19:01,200 --> 00:19:07,080
German and American scientists grappled with petroleum-based formulas to create Buna N and neoprene.

249
00:19:07,660 --> 00:19:14,520
These early substitutes for rubber were precursors to the synthetic compounds that were directly responsible for meeting the military's demands.

250
00:19:15,380 --> 00:19:20,500
Those compounds provided the war machine the rubber resources needed to operate successfully.

251
00:19:20,501 --> 00:19:27,380
The complex process of creating synthetic rubber that was developed during World War II

252
00:19:27,381 --> 00:19:30,960
applied principles that are used in today's synthetic materials.

253
00:19:32,220 --> 00:19:35,800
Creating synthetic rubber is an art form rooted in elementary chemistry.

254
00:19:37,080 --> 00:19:38,920
Atoms group together to form molecules,

255
00:19:39,400 --> 00:19:44,140
and molecules are linked together to assemble strands of complex molecules, known as polymers.

256
00:19:44,780 --> 00:19:48,780
As more polymers are created, the massive strands entangle, much like spaghetti.

257
00:19:49,620 --> 00:19:55,460
Through vulcanization, you can essentially connect all those individual molecules,

258
00:19:55,680 --> 00:19:58,880
practically making one giant molecule of rubber.

259
00:19:59,740 --> 00:20:02,880
And if you can think, if you let a plate of spaghetti sit,

260
00:20:03,520 --> 00:20:06,820
pretty much everywhere the spaghetti touches another strand of spaghetti,

261
00:20:06,920 --> 00:20:07,840
it's going to stick together.

262
00:20:08,460 --> 00:20:12,160
Chemists blend carefully measured ingredients using precise recipes

263
00:20:12,161 --> 00:20:15,020
to create the exact consistency desired.

264
00:20:15,820 --> 00:20:19,840
We've got an example of a very simplistic formula for you

265
00:20:19,841 --> 00:20:25,020
just to demonstrate the different building blocks that go into a rubber compound.

266
00:20:25,580 --> 00:20:28,680
This is the raw synthetic polymer.

267
00:20:28,681 --> 00:20:31,640
This is actually EPDM.

268
00:20:32,020 --> 00:20:35,460
It's an ethylene propylene with a diene monomer.

269
00:20:36,100 --> 00:20:39,680
With this, we're going to incorporate some plasticizer.

270
00:20:40,140 --> 00:20:41,460
It's basically oil.

271
00:20:41,461 --> 00:20:48,300
It's a plasticizer that has a specific property that will extend and soften this batch.

272
00:20:48,660 --> 00:20:51,980
This is an example of the carbon black that's going into the batch.

273
00:20:52,360 --> 00:20:55,040
A very little bit of this goes a very long way.

274
00:20:55,860 --> 00:21:00,620
This is also one of the ingredients, a silicate, a clay-type material.

275
00:21:01,340 --> 00:21:03,840
This material is the curative.

276
00:21:04,460 --> 00:21:08,600
It's sulfur-based, but there's a number of the different colored materials in there

277
00:21:08,601 --> 00:21:10,820
are actually different chemicals.

278
00:21:11,460 --> 00:21:16,920
We've got a little bit of Modern Marvel's Custom Mix 101.

279
00:21:16,921 --> 00:21:22,760
We've taken the materials that we just described and we're actually going to use a technique

280
00:21:22,761 --> 00:21:28,400
called upside down mixing where we're going to load the carbon black, the balance of the

281
00:21:28,401 --> 00:21:36,020
fillers and plasticizer and then load the polymer, the raw synthetic EPDM, last.

282
00:21:36,021 --> 00:21:42,880
the ram, masticate, put this together, and then based on temperature, we will add the

283
00:21:42,881 --> 00:21:46,560
curative to the mix to get a homogeneous mix.

284
00:21:48,260 --> 00:21:54,000
Akin to making pie crust dough, all the ingredients for this exclusive recipe are mashed together

285
00:21:54,001 --> 00:21:55,360
and kneaded until pliable.

286
00:21:55,780 --> 00:22:00,520
Then the mixture drops down to a mill that compresses the rubber into a uniform consistency.

287
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:05,900
It's a little bit of a black art, even the way he's handling that.

288
00:22:06,400 --> 00:22:11,160
The synthetic rubber compound then passes through a second mill, called a calendar,

289
00:22:11,840 --> 00:22:17,320
which squishes the rubber to the desired thickness, then slices it to a prescribed width.

290
00:22:19,960 --> 00:22:24,820
Next, the strips are coated with a solution that will help prevent the rubber from sticking to itself.

291
00:22:25,580 --> 00:22:29,180
The final step for our modern Marvel's custom-blended synthetic rubber

292
00:22:29,181 --> 00:22:33,380
is to drape it over cantilevers so it can cool and dry.

293
00:22:34,500 --> 00:22:37,440
Since World War II, the manufacturers of synthetic rubber

294
00:22:37,441 --> 00:22:39,840
have made great strides in polymer science.

295
00:22:40,380 --> 00:22:44,520
These advancements facilitated the production of an indispensable industrial creation,

296
00:22:44,521 --> 00:22:45,500
the tire.

297
00:22:47,560 --> 00:22:51,820
Wallace H. Carruthers, who headed the team at DuPont that formulated neoprene,

298
00:22:52,240 --> 00:22:54,260
is also credited with creating nylon.

299
00:22:54,820 --> 00:22:58,180
Rubber will return on Modern Marvels.

300
00:23:04,880 --> 00:23:12,980
The massive ramp-up of rubber production to support the military during World War II reached full speed thanks to the support of some muscular machines.

301
00:23:13,760 --> 00:23:22,980
The rubber industry is the only industry in the world that has machines that are just over-designed and over-built, and they'll last a lifetime.

302
00:23:22,981 --> 00:23:23,681
a lifetime.

303
00:23:23,682 --> 00:23:27,900
We've had machines here that are over 100 years old, and we'll go ahead and put in new

304
00:23:27,901 --> 00:23:32,140
bearings, new gears, and a better drive, and send it out for another 100-year production.

305
00:23:32,141 --> 00:23:37,740
At Rubber City Machine in Akron, Ohio, the vintage tools of the trade are restored to their optimal

306
00:23:37,741 --> 00:23:42,196
operating capacity. This machine is a laboratory mill.

307
00:23:42,197 --> 00:23:45,580
By the design of the frames, I could tell

308
00:23:45,581 --> 00:23:47,820
that it was about 100 years old.

309
00:23:48,980 --> 00:23:51,220
This is a French oil mill press.

310
00:23:51,340 --> 00:23:52,860
It's made in Pickwell, Ohio.

311
00:23:53,340 --> 00:23:54,760
Originally, this probably could be used

312
00:23:54,761 --> 00:23:57,000
for making hard rubber battery cases

313
00:23:57,001 --> 00:23:58,520
for automobiles and motorcycles.

314
00:23:59,060 --> 00:24:01,560
This vintage is about 1935, 1940.

315
00:24:02,240 --> 00:24:03,300
It looks really bad,

316
00:24:03,640 --> 00:24:05,280
but it'll go out looking like a new press.

317
00:24:06,480 --> 00:24:08,120
This is a two-row mill.

318
00:24:08,260 --> 00:24:09,400
This is definitely a gem.

319
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:10,860
It was built prior to 1900.

320
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:12,660
We could regrind the rolls,

321
00:24:12,780 --> 00:24:14,680
put in new bronze bearings.

322
00:24:15,360 --> 00:24:19,480
new gears, of course, and rebuild this, put a new drive on it,

323
00:24:19,840 --> 00:24:22,480
and it would go out and last another 100 years.

324
00:24:22,940 --> 00:24:25,920
The durability and brute strength of equipment like this

325
00:24:25,921 --> 00:24:29,240
helped make it possible to overcome wartime rubber shortages.

326
00:24:30,700 --> 00:24:34,960
But peace offered no slowdown in the production of natural and synthetic rubber.

327
00:24:35,620 --> 00:24:39,620
The bottom line is the war was a driver for an awful lot of things in this country.

328
00:24:40,180 --> 00:24:42,520
The economy boomed after the war for many reasons,

329
00:24:42,521 --> 00:24:47,640
not the least of which was all those materials that were developed in support of that war machine

330
00:24:47,641 --> 00:24:49,940
were now able to be used by the consumer.

331
00:24:50,720 --> 00:24:55,420
There was a demand for new products and labor-saving products,

332
00:24:55,560 --> 00:25:00,200
especially making the quality of life easier, less work for the household.

333
00:25:00,720 --> 00:25:04,420
We had the concept of the new family unit that was going to have a car,

334
00:25:04,560 --> 00:25:06,180
was going to have perhaps two cars.

335
00:25:06,700 --> 00:25:09,800
And each new car was equipped with five new tires.

336
00:25:10,800 --> 00:25:15,740
The rapid rise in tire sales was good news for the small midwestern town of Akron, Ohio.

337
00:25:16,740 --> 00:25:23,900
As the tire industry sort of boomed with the rise of the automobile and building of more highways and all of that,

338
00:25:24,500 --> 00:25:30,400
most of the Akron rubber companies focused on tires as their main moneymaker.

339
00:25:30,740 --> 00:25:35,120
Innovations in tire technology have always been the best way to bolster tire sales.

340
00:25:36,120 --> 00:25:40,940
The early balloon designs with an inner tube gave way to the tubeless style,

341
00:25:41,180 --> 00:25:46,640
with a new thin inner liner molded into the tire to retain the air and support the tire.

342
00:25:47,240 --> 00:25:49,600
This design was called bias ply tires,

343
00:25:50,200 --> 00:25:55,700
because reinforced rubber belts were wrapped along the bias, or direction of travel, for strength.

344
00:25:56,580 --> 00:26:00,400
Then sidewall and tread layers were molded as exterior surfaces.

345
00:26:01,300 --> 00:26:08,400
Bias tires, which were the typical construction feature up to the early 70s, were very fuel inefficient.

346
00:26:09,120 --> 00:26:14,640
In 1948, efficiency was engineered into a radical new design, the radial tire.

347
00:26:14,641 --> 00:26:18,400
The inventor of the radial tire, his name is Marius Mignot.

348
00:26:19,480 --> 00:26:25,900
And what's interesting is that Marius Mignot was hired into Michelin actually to work in the printing department.

349
00:26:26,820 --> 00:26:33,120
But Michelin gave him the opportunity to work in the area of tire design and tire engineering.

350
00:26:33,900 --> 00:26:35,980
And as a result of that, we have the radial tire.

351
00:26:36,800 --> 00:26:41,380
The radial design featured reinforced belts that radiate in a circular pattern,

352
00:26:42,040 --> 00:26:44,580
90 degrees perpendicular to the direction of travel.

353
00:26:45,340 --> 00:26:50,180
This configuration provided strength across the entire surface of the sidewalls and tread layer

354
00:26:50,181 --> 00:26:52,660
and greatly improved efficiency.

355
00:26:52,661 --> 00:26:58,140
But the Akron tire makers were reluctant to accept the revolutionary design.

356
00:26:59,040 --> 00:27:05,140
For the next 20 years, they would mold innovative fabrics with rubber to enhance bias tires.

357
00:27:05,640 --> 00:27:15,800
The first 50 years of the tire industry was about a bias configuration, cross-ply tire, that would last you 10,000 or 12,000 miles.

358
00:27:15,801 --> 00:27:27,247
As we moved into the 60s, we invented our polyglass tire, which was a reinforced tread area with fiberglass belts under the tread area, which raised the whole

359
00:27:27,248 --> 00:27:34,420
performance level to now you're talking 35,000, 40,000 miles was an expected life in a biased tire.

360
00:27:35,240 --> 00:27:43,080
Not until the oil crisis of the 1970s and the demand for fuel-efficient automobiles did the tire makers adopt the radial tire.

361
00:27:44,020 --> 00:27:46,820
The design that changed tire construction for good.

362
00:27:48,140 --> 00:27:55,280
The construction features of today's radial tire are a radial body ply, generally made out of polyester,

363
00:27:56,540 --> 00:28:01,940
a bead filler material, which allows us to adjust the stiffnesses of the sidewall of the tire,

364
00:28:02,700 --> 00:28:04,780
two steel belts in general.

365
00:28:05,340 --> 00:28:07,160
These steel belts are added by us.

366
00:28:07,161 --> 00:28:13,280
They're on angles opposite to each other to give the tire the ability to generate cornering force,

367
00:28:13,460 --> 00:28:21,740
as well as a cap line material and tread rubber that translate all of the behaviors of this structure to the road surface.

368
00:28:22,220 --> 00:28:28,060
The tread region, the tread rubber, is really where the technology advancements over the last several years have come from.

369
00:28:28,560 --> 00:28:35,540
And that's really from polymer evolution, from the ability to blend different synthetic rubbers with other naturally occurring materials

370
00:28:35,541 --> 00:28:38,340
to produce the overall performance of the tire.

371
00:28:39,180 --> 00:28:43,120
Processing these high-tech, highly secret, and proprietary rubber compounds

372
00:28:43,121 --> 00:28:45,560
is done at state-of-the-art facilities,

373
00:28:46,020 --> 00:28:48,460
like the Michelin plant in Greenville, South Carolina.

374
00:28:48,980 --> 00:28:51,480
Most of the rubber, the elastomer that we receive,

375
00:28:51,620 --> 00:28:55,320
comes either by rail cars you see behind me here, or by truck,

376
00:28:55,940 --> 00:28:58,540
coming either from domestic production

377
00:28:58,541 --> 00:29:01,760
or, in the case of the natural rubber, coming largely from Singapore.

378
00:29:02,360 --> 00:29:04,160
This natural color is a dark tan.

379
00:29:04,720 --> 00:29:06,520
Also, you'll notice the elastic properties.

380
00:29:06,660 --> 00:29:09,080
You see how stretchy it is, how soft it is.

381
00:29:10,060 --> 00:29:12,520
Those are part of the properties of natural rubber.

382
00:29:13,060 --> 00:29:18,800
And then on this table, we have examples of synthetic rubbers, synthetic elastomers.

383
00:29:18,920 --> 00:29:24,300
This one is stearine butadiene, which is mainly going to be used to improve traction.

384
00:29:25,080 --> 00:29:27,680
This one is polybutadiene, or PBR.

385
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,780
It's going to be mainly used to improve rolling resistance.

386
00:29:30,781 --> 00:29:36,940
and this is yet another form of natural rubber which would be used mainly to improve endurance.

387
00:29:37,740 --> 00:29:43,340
In this factory, we manufacture about 100 different recipes or formulas.

388
00:29:43,940 --> 00:29:49,000
If you think about a tire, the properties that you need in the tread block of a tire

389
00:29:49,001 --> 00:29:54,500
are not at all the same as the properties you need in the inner liner or in the sidewall.

390
00:29:55,780 --> 00:29:59,940
Chemicals and additives are precisely mixed with rubber to the specifications of a recipe.

391
00:30:00,780 --> 00:30:06,060
These chemicals are weighed on the machine behind me to very precise weight tolerance.

392
00:30:06,800 --> 00:30:13,600
And just as an example, here you have an antioxidant, which protects the tire from attack by oxidation.

393
00:30:14,500 --> 00:30:17,680
This is a resin. It helps it to adhere to the metal.

394
00:30:18,900 --> 00:30:25,060
This product is a wax that helps in the processing of the rubber and also helps the tire to stay black.

395
00:30:25,460 --> 00:30:28,180
This is another antioxidant.

396
00:30:28,181 --> 00:30:35,880
This product is an anti-ozon it, so same purpose except this time to shield the tire from attack by ozone.

397
00:30:36,260 --> 00:30:41,780
You would have anywhere from three to sometimes up to eight of these different chemicals in a given formulation,

398
00:30:42,360 --> 00:30:48,600
but when you add it all together, it would only represent three or four total percent of the weight of the rubber compound.

399
00:30:49,960 --> 00:30:52,980
Tons of natural rubber is chopped, tumbled, and washed

400
00:30:52,981 --> 00:30:57,880
as it makes its way across the massive plant toward the mixing machines and mills.

401
00:30:58,180 --> 00:31:03,040
After passing through the secondary mill and calendar,

402
00:31:03,480 --> 00:31:07,540
the compound is fully homogenized and identified with a recipe code.

403
00:31:09,600 --> 00:31:13,580
Once cooled, it is folded and palletized for transportation to the tire plant.

404
00:31:15,020 --> 00:31:19,600
You know, the rubber industry is no longer the dirty, filthy industry that it used to be in the old days.

405
00:31:19,840 --> 00:31:22,440
In fact, you can see, this is a very clean place to work.

406
00:31:22,780 --> 00:31:25,620
It's a very high-tech, high-automation industry.

407
00:31:25,621 --> 00:31:31,560
The same is true for the tire manufacturing plants, where computers and robotic operations

408
00:31:31,561 --> 00:31:36,200
build radial tires from the inside out.

409
00:31:36,201 --> 00:31:43,580
Step by step, layer by layer, tires are assembled with a myriad of rubber compounds, starting

410
00:31:43,581 --> 00:31:52,380
with the inner liner, the steel belted plies, sidewall supports, and bead wire.

411
00:31:52,381 --> 00:31:56,340
the assembly is automatically inflated so that a tread layer can be applied.

412
00:31:57,980 --> 00:32:03,720
Next, this uncured tire is placed in a mold where it is subjected to extreme heat and pressure

413
00:32:03,721 --> 00:32:09,160
to vulcanize all the elements. In the final production phase, each and every tire is

414
00:32:09,161 --> 00:32:14,080
quality controlled by technicians who must approve every aspect of the automated process.

415
00:32:14,980 --> 00:32:18,700
Random samples are frequently pulled from the line for spot inspection and testing.

416
00:32:18,701 --> 00:32:25,920
The basic process of mixing natural and synthetic rubber with other chemicals and additives into a pliable compound

417
00:32:25,921 --> 00:32:31,620
that can be molded, injected, compressed, or extruded isn't just used in making tires.

418
00:32:32,420 --> 00:32:36,140
It also creates many other products that surround us daily.

419
00:32:38,720 --> 00:32:44,060
There are approximately 165 pounds of rubber in the average passenger car.

420
00:32:44,860 --> 00:32:48,260
Rubber will return on Modern Marvels.

421
00:32:48,700 --> 00:32:58,760
The 1960s saw many new uses for rubber.

422
00:32:59,660 --> 00:33:00,600
In the home.

423
00:33:01,540 --> 00:33:02,580
On the job.

424
00:33:03,400 --> 00:33:04,440
And at play.

425
00:33:05,940 --> 00:33:10,520
In some cases, rubber literally became the foundation of where we lived and worked.

426
00:33:12,300 --> 00:33:16,400
Albany Court was built in London above the St. James Underground Rail Station.

427
00:33:16,401 --> 00:33:22,500
It was the first building to rest on natural rubber anti-vibration mounts.

428
00:33:22,860 --> 00:33:26,900
As far as some of the other extremes on vibration control for rubber,

429
00:33:27,620 --> 00:33:31,760
some of the applications that are in use today are for isolating buildings,

430
00:33:32,340 --> 00:33:36,700
where you would suspend or support the building on rubber mounts,

431
00:33:36,920 --> 00:33:41,300
and if an earthquake happened, then basically the earthquake will shake,

432
00:33:41,301 --> 00:33:46,620
and it would isolate the building from falling down in the earthquake.

433
00:33:47,120 --> 00:33:52,500
The elastic nature of the rubber allows the motion of the earth to move at a different rate than the building.

434
00:33:53,800 --> 00:33:57,040
Rubber mounts also dampen the motion felt on some bridges.

435
00:33:58,820 --> 00:34:01,360
Rubber is also found atop most commercial buildings.

436
00:34:02,760 --> 00:34:07,960
Hot molten rubber is brushed across roof surfaces to form a water-repellent protective barrier.

437
00:34:08,700 --> 00:34:13,900
Rubber is used to seal things so that either air or liquid does not get in.

438
00:34:13,901 --> 00:34:18,480
The uses of rubber are as varied as the imagination.

439
00:34:19,320 --> 00:34:24,520
They're pretty much rubber components in almost everything that a person uses every day.

440
00:34:25,040 --> 00:34:32,520
That includes hand tools, kitchen appliances, weather stripping around doors and windows, and sporting goods.

441
00:34:33,320 --> 00:34:39,720
Even trains and trucks rely on rubber cushions in their suspension systems to maintain stability under heavy loads.

442
00:34:41,160 --> 00:34:49,320
Often, cargo is wrapped in a rubberized film wrap as a strong, easy-to-use, and cost-effective way to protect freight during transportation.

443
00:34:50,220 --> 00:34:58,900
Silicon rubber is huge in the medical industry because silicon rubber was virtually non-reactive in the human body.

444
00:34:58,901 --> 00:35:05,440
So silicone rubber is used to this day for all kinds of medical applications.

445
00:35:05,660 --> 00:35:09,440
If there's something that's implanted in you, it's encapsulated in silicone.

446
00:35:10,080 --> 00:35:15,740
While silicone is usually medically safe, it's not always the best rubber for certain clinical applications.

447
00:35:16,580 --> 00:35:22,520
Some industry experts are hopeful about the prospect of using the latex that comes from a plant called Waiuli.

448
00:35:22,521 --> 00:35:28,660
It turns out that Waiuli latex is considered to be hypoallergenic.

449
00:35:28,900 --> 00:35:31,100
compared to Havia latex.

450
00:35:31,440 --> 00:35:39,380
In the healthcare industry, there is a crisis due to allergic reactions to natural latex products such as gloves.

451
00:35:39,920 --> 00:35:48,420
For example, a dental technician may not be able to use the latex gloves that are typically used because of a severe allergic reaction.

452
00:35:48,960 --> 00:35:52,140
Waiuli may be a significant remedy for some medical situations,

453
00:35:52,560 --> 00:35:57,460
but it could play an even greater role as a major contributor to the world's natural rubber supply.

454
00:35:57,461 --> 00:35:59,722
An important benefit for the U.S.

455
00:35:59,723 --> 00:36:03,560
is that Waiuli is grown in the deserts of North America.

456
00:36:05,240 --> 00:36:07,760
Waiuli is a very interesting plant.

457
00:36:08,060 --> 00:36:13,020
It's a small desert shrub that's native to West Texas and northern Mexico.

458
00:36:14,080 --> 00:36:16,740
It's a very unassuming-looking plant.

459
00:36:16,960 --> 00:36:19,920
If you drove by it, you wouldn't even give it a second look.

460
00:36:20,480 --> 00:36:23,500
It's about three feet tall and resembles sagebrush.

461
00:36:23,501 --> 00:36:31,760
But what makes it an interesting plant is that it contains the same natural rubber as the tropical tree that grows in the Far East.

462
00:36:33,120 --> 00:36:39,740
Wyuli is one of the many latex-bearing plants that was studied extensively as an alternative resource during the rubber shortage of World War II.

463
00:36:40,600 --> 00:36:50,680
The Department of Defense leased land from farmers in three states in the southwest to grow about 32,000 acres of wyuli during the war.

464
00:36:50,681 --> 00:36:56,420
At the end of World War II, the Waiuli that was growing in the southwest was plowed under.

465
00:36:57,840 --> 00:37:06,080
And that was pretty much the end of any major Waiuli effort for a number of years until the oil embargo in the 1970s.

466
00:37:07,160 --> 00:37:16,440
Policymakers in Washington decided that we needed to take another look at critical agriculture materials that we would import dependent on.

467
00:37:17,340 --> 00:37:19,660
And natural rubber was one of those materials.

468
00:37:19,661 --> 00:37:28,260
The quality of the Waiuli latex is marketable, but the processing of the plant has yet to provide sufficient yields to make Waiuli a valuable commodity.

469
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:30,060
But that's changing.

470
00:37:30,620 --> 00:37:36,800
In Waiuli, the rubber latex is contained in individual cells, so you can't tap it.

471
00:37:36,860 --> 00:37:41,340
You have to harvest the entire shrub, grind it up, and extract the latex.

472
00:37:41,620 --> 00:37:47,040
Because of the economics, imported rubber has always been relatively inexpensive.

473
00:37:47,041 --> 00:38:00,180
But now, because of this hypoallergenic quality of the Waiuli latex, products can command the premium price to make it a commercial success.

474
00:38:00,660 --> 00:38:01,360
The U.S.

475
00:38:01,361 --> 00:38:06,700
Department of Agriculture continues to research Waiuli as a domestically grown source of natural rubber.

476
00:38:07,360 --> 00:38:08,600
I think it's here to stay.

477
00:38:08,601 --> 00:38:18,820
we're starting to realize that we need to be a little more diverse in our agricultural base as well as our industrial base.

478
00:38:19,260 --> 00:38:26,940
From an agricultural perspective, Waiuli offers an opportunity for a new crop beyond the conventional crops that we grow in this country.

479
00:38:27,340 --> 00:38:33,400
From an industrial base perspective, we will have a domestic source of the critical and strategic material.

480
00:38:33,401 --> 00:38:39,000
Alternate sources of latex will help to assure the development of new rubber products.

481
00:38:39,900 --> 00:38:44,720
But it's the old rubber products that have been difficult to inventory and destroy.

482
00:38:47,400 --> 00:38:52,880
Building contractors used 41 miles of butyl rubber to seal the joints of the Houston Astrodome.

483
00:38:54,660 --> 00:38:56,840
Rubber will return on Modern Marvels.

484
00:39:03,400 --> 00:39:08,400
Recycling tires back into tires is not a viable alternative.

485
00:39:08,401 --> 00:39:15,400
The downside about rubber that's cured is that you can't re-melt it and you can't reuse it.

486
00:39:15,401 --> 00:39:17,400
You have to grind it up to reuse it.

487
00:39:17,401 --> 00:39:24,400
It's the vulcanization that gives rubber its form, which prevents it from being reduced to its prime elements again.

488
00:39:24,401 --> 00:39:30,400
In the 1970s, factories and kilns burned tires as an alternate fuel source.

489
00:39:30,401 --> 00:39:34,098
source. And we were quite happy with tires as a fuel.

490
00:39:34,099 --> 00:39:36,400
If you substitute tires for coal,

491
00:39:37,020 --> 00:39:43,080
you end up with less sulfur dioxide emissions, less nitrogen oxide emissions.

492
00:39:44,020 --> 00:39:49,800
At best, tires can be ground to a fine powder to be used as filler material in some applications.

493
00:39:51,140 --> 00:39:57,329
Generally, you can use reground material, very finely reground material.

494
00:39:57,330 --> 00:39:58,360
For example,

495
00:39:58,361 --> 00:40:01,040
You use rubber in pavements today, roads.

496
00:40:01,440 --> 00:40:10,480
You can use rubber in artificial football fields, artificial soccer fields, also on tennis courts.

497
00:40:10,800 --> 00:40:14,260
There are many places where you can let the old material be used.

498
00:40:15,940 --> 00:40:20,620
I should raise one other point, too, and a point that most people aren't aware of.

499
00:40:20,680 --> 00:40:25,360
In the last 20 years, we've probably increased the life of the average tire by more than 50%.

500
00:40:25,361 --> 00:40:26,061
per shot.

501
00:40:26,062 --> 00:40:30,760
Tires with a lifetime warranty have become commonplace and may slow the rate of

502
00:40:30,761 --> 00:40:32,787
rubber entering the scrap inventories.

503
00:40:32,788 --> 00:40:35,240
This may also reduce the burden on the natural

504
00:40:35,241 --> 00:40:36,367
rubber plantations.

505
00:40:36,368 --> 00:40:40,340
My understanding is that most of the good rubber growing lands have

506
00:40:40,341 --> 00:40:43,305
been maxed out. There's room for growth in Indonesia.

507
00:40:43,306 --> 00:40:45,040
There's some room for expansion

508
00:40:45,041 --> 00:40:50,340
in Vietnam. There's room for expansion in China. And the Chinese are planting rubber

509
00:40:50,341 --> 00:40:51,111
like mad.

510
00:40:51,112 --> 00:40:58,220
But in general, you see a scenario where rubber production is not going to increase

511
00:40:58,221 --> 00:40:59,960
nearly as much as demand.

512
00:41:03,500 --> 00:41:08,380
The business of growing rubber is always risky due to climatic and botanical conditions,

513
00:41:08,960 --> 00:41:10,940
especially the fungal leaf blight.

514
00:41:11,760 --> 00:41:17,420
One of the curious things about the South American leaf blight is that it's not a problem

515
00:41:17,421 --> 00:41:18,380
until it happens.

516
00:41:18,860 --> 00:41:21,600
And we don't have a lot to go on

517
00:41:21,601 --> 00:41:23,240
to really ascertain

518
00:41:23,241 --> 00:41:26,060
what are the possible consequences

519
00:41:26,061 --> 00:41:28,960
of this disease breaking out in Southeast Asia.

520
00:41:31,380 --> 00:41:33,460
It's not as if the sky is going to fall in

521
00:41:33,461 --> 00:41:35,380
if the disease reaches Southeast Asia.

522
00:41:35,700 --> 00:41:37,100
I mean, it's a fungal parasite.

523
00:41:37,540 --> 00:41:39,300
It can be controlled with fungicides.

524
00:41:39,920 --> 00:41:41,860
The problem is that the big plantations

525
00:41:41,861 --> 00:41:43,880
may indeed have the resources to do so,

526
00:41:43,881 --> 00:41:47,080
but roughly 50% of the rubber production

527
00:41:47,081 --> 00:41:50,020
in a place like Malaysia is coming from small family farms

528
00:41:50,021 --> 00:41:52,960
that do not have that kind of infrastructural support.

529
00:41:54,940 --> 00:41:57,680
Nevertheless, many experts are optimistic

530
00:41:57,681 --> 00:41:59,420
about the outlook for the rubber industry

531
00:41:59,421 --> 00:42:02,700
and are confident elastomers will remain poised

532
00:42:02,701 --> 00:42:04,220
at the leading edge of technology.

533
00:42:07,500 --> 00:42:11,280
And in the future, we can hope to see a time

534
00:42:11,281 --> 00:42:13,800
when nobody will have to worry about having a flat tire.

535
00:42:13,801 --> 00:42:19,120
and now that we've learned to deal with this new science

536
00:42:19,121 --> 00:42:21,980
relatively new science called nanotechnology

537
00:42:21,981 --> 00:42:26,660
the reinforcement of polymers with nanoparticles

538
00:42:26,661 --> 00:42:30,580
has led to a new generation of highly stiff

539
00:42:30,581 --> 00:42:34,480
very lightweight materials that will go into the transportation industry

540
00:42:34,481 --> 00:42:35,760
and the aerospace industry

541
00:42:35,761 --> 00:42:38,480
I see continued evolution of the materials

542
00:42:38,481 --> 00:42:42,180
continued development of new materials that will meet

543
00:42:42,181 --> 00:42:43,700
the new challenges

544
00:42:44,660 --> 00:42:51,820
Another thing that's being done is to add some kind of a controllable feature to the molecule of the rubber itself,

545
00:42:52,440 --> 00:42:58,280
where what you can do is actually make the rubber change shape if you put an electric field on,

546
00:42:58,281 --> 00:43:02,400
and essentially create something that's very much like an artificial muscle.

547
00:43:03,300 --> 00:43:10,920
The prospect of making functional biomechanical tissue from rubber may give future candidates for reconstructive surgery a reason to smile.

548
00:43:10,921 --> 00:43:21,100
There's always search for that significant technology that is going to be a leap rather than an evolution.

549
00:43:21,900 --> 00:43:24,600
I'm sure we'll have those. We've had them along the way.

550
00:43:24,840 --> 00:43:26,640
History says that we're going to have them again.

551
00:43:28,380 --> 00:43:31,660
I don't know what they'll be, but I'm sure we're going to pursue them.

552
00:43:33,580 --> 00:43:37,360
Only time will tell how far rubber technology will evolve.

553
00:43:37,361 --> 00:43:42,820
In our homes, our industry, our science and beyond

554
00:43:42,821 --> 00:43:45,640
Rubber will always be wedged into our existence

555
00:43:45,641 --> 00:43:49,420
And we'll still need the white blood of the forest

556
00:43:49,421 --> 00:43:53,320
Sliced from the hevia tree bark and collected into tiny cups

557
00:43:53,321 --> 00:43:56,760
Just as it was 400 years ago

